**Current versus accepted.** Alpha scaffolds use declaration-owned `#Config of T`
inputs with grouped parenthesised config and reserved page metadata. The accepted
`$config`/`$project`/`$html_builder` and root-only `$page` forms are queued.
`moth new` does not emit them yet.

A Moth project is the directory you select. That directory is both the build
root and the source root. There is no implicit `src/` folder.

### Accepted starter config

The queued directive form leaves version optional and has no `entry_root`:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $project(
        name = "my_site",
    )

    $html_builder
]

### Where things live

- `config.moth` at the selected root is optional and controls build settings.
- Everything below the selected root is source unless it is excluded or is a generated hidden output.
- `_dev/` and `_release/` are generated output. Do not edit them.

### One root file per module directory

Each source directory can have one root file. An `@*.moth` root creates a normal
module. The text after `@` stays cosmetic, so `@page.moth`, `@api.moth` and
`@mod.moth` all create the same root role. A root becomes an HTML artefact only
when it declares a root-only page:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $page
]

The current scaffold's `@page.moth` filename doesn't mean it already has this
accepted page syntax.

### Shared scoped packages

A `+*.moth` root creates a scoped support package instead of a normal module.
For example, `shared/+package.moth` creates `@shared`. Its owner module, normal
sibling modules and their descendants may depend on it. Other support packages
in the same scope and the package's private implementation subtree cannot. A
directory cannot contain both `@*.moth` and `+*.moth` roots.

### Outputs are generated

The build command creates hidden output folders. They are not source. The
builder cleans up stale artefacts automatically.
